![](/images/articles/cat-revisited.jpeg)

Three-and-a-half years ago, after listening to a few songs with my then-18-month-old daughter Maya, I posted this article, [On the Cat in the Hat and Women Musicians](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/cat-hat-women-musicians-steve-bamberger/). I argued that I’d rather Maya listen to the music I heard that morning (Meghan Trainor, Katie Perry, Taylor Swift) than to the female artists that were top-of-mind for me 20 and 30 years ago (Madonna, Cyndi Lauper, Alanis Morrisette).

Maya turned five last month, and is old enough to be introducing ME to music. One of her favorites is [Fight Song by Rachel Platten](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fight_Song_(Rachel_Platten_song)). I had forgotten that Platten was recommended to me in comments on my original article by my friend Melissa “Jasmine” Carson. Imagine my surprise when, attending a recent concert for a cappella band Pentatonix at Oracle Arena, Rachel Platten turned out to be the opening act.

Platten spoke emotionally about having a baby and sang “You Belong.” It was the first night of the tour, and she talked about how earlier that day, she had been sobbing in the corner. She was doubting that she could handle going on the road with a new baby, who was sleeping soundly in the bus outside. Having filled the stadium of 12,000 with raw vulnerable emotion, she closed with “Fight Song.” And she rocked the house.

This is my fight song / Take back my life song / Prove I'm alright song / My powers turned on / Starting right now I'll be strong / I'll play my fight song

I reread my post from a few years ago, and I think it holds up. If anything, I am more convinced that this new generation of women artists is different than what has come before: unapologetically feminine, unusually open, and unquestionably strong.

What an optimistic time to raise a daughter.
